==Gameplay Overview==
''(NOTE: Although other games have called him “Kim”, CotW refers to this character as “Kaphwan” in-game, to differentiate him from his two sons. That name will be used throughout this overview.)''

After a long absence from ''Fatal Fury'', Kim Kaphwan is finally back! This staple of SNK fighting games is a close-range pressure character whose ability to string together damage and blockstrings is matched by few, if any, in the cast.

While Jae Hoon is about defense and footsies, and Dong Hwan is about acrobatics and mix-ups, Kaphwan is about pressure and punishment. He is absolutely relentless up close, with good normals, strong specials, and the return of his Haki Kyaku stomp. This once-infamous special lets Kaphwan stomp multiple times in a row, locking down the opponent, or go into a stance whose options enable high/low mix, call out mashing, or even cash out into high damage with a Counter Hit. You can use these techniques and the threat of an invincible reversal to intimidate the opponent into clamming up, giving you free reign to hammer away at their guard and REV gauge.

Kaphwan has one other unique quirk: he’s uniquely strong in the air, too! Boasting a cancellable air heavy, a strong divekick special in Hishou Kyaku, a powerful air-to-air move in Kuusen Kyaku, and an air super, Kaphwan can simply rule out jumping as a means of escaping his pressure, or call out certain anti-airs. Add on top of that his flashkick and good normals for hop checking, and the skies are all but off-limits against Kaphwan.

Kaphwan’s main issue lies in struggling to gain or retain momentum without resources. With no long-range normals, neutral skips, or anti-fireball moves of any kind, you may have to commit hard to get in on more passive opponents. He also has a tendency to reset to neutral inconveniently often due to the poor advantage on his soft knockdowns. Once he’s in, though, it’s up to the player to make it count with his suffocating offense, obliterating opponents’ health bars and patience in the name of that sweet justice.

{{StrengthsAndWeaknesses
| intro = Kim Kaphwan is a high-offense pressure character who metes out endless justice with his solid normals and lengthy attack sequences.
| pros=
* '''Relentless Attacker''': Kaphwan’s greatest strength lies in his seemingly endless blockstrings and offense. Good frame data, a divekick, a strong stomp into stance options, and more mean Kaphwan can string together a long-lasting barrage of chip damage and frame traps, using the Haki Kyaku stance for stagger pressure to beat Just Defend. Many of his special moves have easy Counter Hit conversions, as well.
* '''Aerial Threat''': Access to two dedicated aerial specials, an air super, and a juggling, cancellable j.C make Kaphwan a threatening presence against grounded and airborne opponent alike. He can convert into these from his Flashkick and his REV divekick, giving him impressive anti-air or even air-to-ground damage.
* '''High Damage''': Between higher-than-average damage on his normals and surprisingly freeform combo structure, Kaphwan can rack up some incredible damage off of any hit. Thanks to flashkick brake, Kaphwan even gets surprising damage from his lights.
* '''Flashkick''': Having a flashkick instead of a DP allows Kaphwan’s players to use its invincibility more reactively. When braking the Heavy version, Kaphwan can destroy opponents for jumping in on him while he has charge, even if they’re about to cross him up.
* '''Meter Build''': Thanks to his long pressure sequences, Kaphwan can build more meter on average when it’s his turn than other characters do. This is important as Kaphwan’s supers give him many things he otherwise doesn’t have, like invincibility without charge, a hard knockdown, or long-range punishment.
| cons=
* '''Weak Okizeme''': All of Kaphwan’s meterless enders lack substantial knockdown advantage, meaning he’ll struggle to safely chase or cover wakeup rolls without a hard read. Without REV Blow or a super, Kaphwan will reset to neutral more often than an offensive character would like. 
* '''Zoned Out''': Kaphwan is at his strongest up close, but he lacks ways to skip neutral or deal with fireballs to get up close quickly. He has to get in the old-fashioned way, which can pose problems against zoners like Kain or Krauser.
* '''Flashkick''': Due to requiring charge for his invincible reversal, Kaphwan can’t quickly reposition before anti-airing with it, nor can he use it immediately after blocking an overhead or jump-in. Charging it can leave you vulnerable to overheads and throws.
}}

==Normals==
===Far Standing Normals===
====Far A====
{{FrameDataCargo-COTW
|moveId=kim_fA
|description=* Chains from other lights. Technically chains to itself, but this chain whiffs more often than not
* Unusually high damage for a far A
Standard hop-checking punch attack with decent range and 5f startup, making it your optimal button to mash. 

At 40 damage, it's one of the highest-damaging A normals in the game despite its fast startup. The only downside is its lack of chaining options compared to 2A. You can't charge while pressing it, but once you've practiced charge buffering, you can get around that. 
}}

====Far B====
{{FrameDataCargo-COTW
|moveId=kim_fB
|description=* Deceptively large range and fast for a far B
* Kaphwan's most consistent cancel to heavy flashkick brake
* Moves Kaphwan forward a bit on startup, but if you don't buffer anything during the recovery, he will move back to where he started the move 
This normal can do so much that it acts as Kaphwan's swiss army knife. 

Not only is it fast for a far B, its hitbox is huge, it chains easily from his crouching lights, it special cancels, and with charge buffering, it allows you to do three lights to flashkick, even from two 2Bs. At nearly all ranges, it creates a spacing trap into far C (although crouching normals can check it). There's almost nothing this normal can't do, really, though it's worse on whiff than your average light normal.

On Counter Hit or Wild Punish, it combos to 214BD, making it incredible as a counterpoke if you have REV to spare.
}}

====Far C====
{{FrameDataCargo-COTW
|moveId=kim_fC
|description=* Wild Punish +19 (+23 when feint cancelled)
* Kaphwan's longest-ranged normal that still special cancels
* Loses some range vs crouching characters, causing the feint cancel link from close C to whiff on crouch
A snappy, decently-ranged kick with good size and forward movement, great for whiff punishes, controlling space, and the like. It's kind of like Terry's far C.

Most notable about this normal is its whopping 90 damage and ability to combo to flashkick brake at max range (unlike 2C), but with the caveat of whiffing on crouch past a certain range. As a result, you will need to forgo this normal in favor of 2C in combos on a crouching opponent. 
}}

====Far D====
{{FrameDataCargo-COTW
|moveId=kim_fD
|description=
* Wild Punish +22
* Fast, two-hitting and with huge range on the second kick, featuring target combo and super cancel options
* The second kick always comes out, making it a good surprise poke, though it's awful on whiff
Fast for a far heavy while also having great range, this double kick is your best heavy for scoring Wild Punishes. Tag them with this if you see them whiff something laggy. Take care not to use this button too recklessly, as it's pretty bad on whiff itself while also being vulnerable to hops.

If both hits are blocked, Kaphwan will always be in range to get mixed up by the opponent, or outright punished if they have a grab super. If you’re trying to space out an opponent, use his other normals instead.
}}

===Close Standing Normals===
====Close A====
{{FrameDataCargo-COTW
|moveId=kim_clA
|description=* Kaphwan's fastest attack
* Used to stop hops, in light confirms, or to perform his Combination Attack
* Chains from both crouching normals point-blank
The standard fast jab is the main move Kaphwan will mash or stop hops with, especially since far A is only a frame slower. Useful to go into his Combination Attack as well, as this  is Kaphwan's only way of comboing to his REV Blow from lights without spending REV. 

}}
====Close B====
{{FrameDataCargo-COTW
|moveId=kim_clB
|description=* Two-hitting attack with special and target combo cancel options from the second hit
* Long activation range for a light means you can chain into it from either crouching normal or his close A
* Links to far B on hit, or 2C on Counter Hit / Wild Punish
* Cannot be feint cancelled, as target combo has higher input priority
Another two-hit attack with great advantage whether it hits or is blocked. This second hit gives you inherent time to charge, making it much better on hit than your other lights thanks to the linking routes to flashkick brake. 
}}

====Close C====
{{FrameDataCargo-COTW
|moveId=kim_clC
|description=* Wild Punish +26 (+29 when feint cancelled)
* Slightly slower than 2C, and shorter activation range than close D, but is your best heavy on hit and block
* Links to far B on hit, even without feint cancel
Kaphwan's strongest heavy button by many metrics, although its use is limited by its proximity requirement. On hit when feint cancelled, links into far C, which leads into all your strongest combos, or even sweep for a surprise knockdown. It also frame traps to almost every other normal you have on block. 
}}

====Close D====
{{FrameDataCargo-COTW
|name=close D
|moveId=kim_clD
|input=cl.D
|description=* Wild Punish +24 (+28 when feint cancelled)
* Forces stand on hit
* Long proximity range allows it to work just outside of throw range
* The first hit reaches VERY high, making it useful as a situational anti-air
* Cancels to supers on either hit, but to specials on the second hit only
A pretty good offensive button, as they go, thanks to its two hits, high vertical hitbox, and consistent link on feint cancel giving you a route to 2C for charge-building. Thanks to reaching just beyond throw range, it's a powerful button in shimmies.

On hit against airborne opponents, it will not cancel to special moves due to the second hit whiffing. This allows you to option-select special cancels, as you won't whiff a special move when tagging a hop or backdash. This utility along with its other strengths arguably make Kaphwan's cl.D his most reliable combo starter.
}}

